Step 2: The Universal Extension Audit (Unmasking Fakes)
Data exfiltration often involves packing secrets into a ZIP or RAR archive, and then changing the extension 
to .jpg, .pdf, or .txt so a casual glance misses it

find . -type f -exec file {} + | grep -iE "zip|archive|executable|elf" | grep -vE "\.(zip|rar|7z|tar|gz|exe)"

What this targets: It looks at the actual internal file signatures (magic bytes). If it finds an archive
structure (zip|archive), but the filename extension does not match standard archive extensions
(-vE "\.(zip...)"), it screams an alert.
Real-world outcome: If the user renamed stolen_database.zip to vacation_photo.jpg, this one command catches 
them instantly.

Step 3: The Threat-Hunting Keyword Sweep (Finding Passwords & Keys)
If an employee is exfiltrating data, they usually leave behind staging notes, scripts, or—as in this challenge—
a password text file. They might name it notes.txt, or embed the password text right inside an innocent-looking 
asset

find . -type f -exec sh -c 'strings "$1" | grep -riIE "password|exfiltrat|secret|{.*}" && echo "--> Found in: $1"' _ {} \;

Why this is powerful: It automatically extracts ASCII text (strings) from every single file 
(whether it's an email, a PDF, a JPEG, or a log) and searches for your target keywords.
Real-world outcome: This single line would have processed form1.jpg, extracted the word "password", and printed 
it on your screen in less than a second. No guessing, no jumping folders.

Step 4: The Steganography Triage (Checking Mass Media)
In exfiltration, employees sometimes use steganography to sneak corporate data out through public channels
(like uploading an image to a forum or emailing a picture to their personal account).Since i found images on the
drive during Step 1, you don't guess which ones are bad. I mass-audit them for anomaly indicators like inflated
file size or appended data

# 1. Instantly find and sort the top 5 heaviest images on the system
find . -type f \( -name "*.jpg" -o -name "*.png" \) -exec ls -lh {} + | sort -hr -k 5 | head -n 5

# 2. Extract signatures from ALL images to see if archives are hidden inside them
binwalk *

Real-world outcome: laptop.jpg and office 2.jpg immediately flag as abnormally large or show appended zip 
data in binwalk.

